  "intro": {
    "text": "Text in Python is called a **string** (type **str**). You can write it with double quotes `\"hi\"` or single quotes `'hi'`; they mean the same. Use the other kind when the text itself has a quote: `\"it's\"`.\n\n`+` joins two strings into one: `\"sun\" + \"set\"` is `\"sunset\"`. It adds **no space** for you.\n\n`*` with a whole number repeats: `\"ha\" * 3` is `\"hahaha\"`.\n\n`len(text)` counts the characters, spaces included.\n\nA backslash starts a special character: `\\n` is a new line and `\\t` is a tab. Each counts as **one** character. The **cells** panel shows every character in its own box, numbered from 0.",
    "analogy": "A string is like a row of letter tiles. + puts two rows end to end, * lays the same row down several times, and len counts the tiles. A space is a tile too, just a blank one."
  },
